  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Direct human absolute or estimated oral bioavailability values for the most transferable steroid contexts are below 20%: testosterone 2–8% (C01), abiraterone acetate <10% fasted (C07, C14, C20), testosterone undecanoate 6.8 ± 3.3% (C10, C22), thienorphine very low (C15), mometasone <1% (C09), and fulvestrant low/poor (C04, C16, C18, C19). These support F < 20% for the query's steroid-phenol scaffold.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C07","C14","C20","C10","C22","C15","C09","C04","C16","C18","C19"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall evidence shows presystemic loss for structurally related steroid phenols: estradiol undergoes initial intestinal metabolism to glucuronide/sulfate conjugates (C67); ethynyl estradiol is a P-gp substrate with efflux ratio ~11 collapsing to 1.2 with inhibitor (C66); estriol shows P-gp-mediated efflux (ER 3.2 to 1.3 with inhibitor, C73; ER ~1 with GF120918, C72). This supports low F via intestinal metabolism and efflux.",
      "card_ids": ["C67","C66","C73","C72"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records for related steroid phenols are not dose-normalized and cannot independently establish F; the low pg/mL Cmax values (estradiol 13.3 pg/mL after 0.625 mg, C50; ethinylestradiol 58.5–104 pg/mL after 0.03 mg, C39/C43/C49; levonorgestrel 8.25 pg/mL, C41) do not provide affirmative evidence for F ≥ 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C50","C39","C43","C49","C41"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 solubility records show very low aqueous solubility for related steroids (estradiol very low, C58; danazol ~0.6–10 µg/mL, C51/C53/C55/C56/C57/C61; progesterone 12 µg/mL, C62), supporting an absorption limitation; high Caco-2 permeability for testosterone (C52) does not overcome first-pass loss.",
      "card_ids": ["C58","C51","C53","C55","C56","C57","C61","C62","C52"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Positive-sounding relative or non-absolute statements (thienorphine better than buprenorphine, C24/C25; DMAU orally bioavailable, C36; Casodex 72% at 1 mg/kg in male rats, C38) and L5 high-permeability records for unrelated molecules (morphine C70, naloxone C74, glyceollin C71, THC SMEDDS C63) are different molecules/species/doses/formulations or non-absolute, and do not establish F ≥ 20% for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C24","C25","C36","C38","C70","C74","C71","C63"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C66","C67","C72","C73"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule itself; the low prediction relies on transfer from structurally related steroid contexts.",
    "L5 efflux/metabolism records are for estradiol, ethynyl estradiol, and estriol, not the query; assay systems (Caco-2, MDR1-MDCK, LLC-PK1) and inhibitor conditions differ from in vivo oral absorption.",
    "No query-specific intestinal metabolism or efflux data are available; phenol conjugation and P-gp substrate status are inferred but not directly measured.",
    "L3 exposure records report Cmax but not bioavailability fractions and are not dose-normalized, so they cannot independently establish F relative to the 20% threshold.",
    "Some records are relative comparisons or involve different molecules, species, doses, or formulations (C24, C25, C36, C38, C63, C70, C71, C74), limiting their transferability.",
    "Solubility records are for other steroids; the query's exact aqueous solubility and dissolution behavior are not measured."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted low (F < 20%) because the most transferable steroid contexts report low absolute oral bioavailability (testosterone 2-8%, abiraterone <10%, testosterone undecanoate 6.8%, thienorphine very low, mometasone <1%, fulvestrant low), and L5 gut-wall evidence shows intestinal metabolism and P-gp efflux for structurally related steroid phenols (estradiol, ethynyl estradiol, estriol), supporting presystemic loss; L3 exposure is only pg/mL-level and not dose-normalized, L4 shows very low aqueous solubility for related steroids, and no affirmative evidence supports F ≥ 20%."
}

